============================================================
  HUMAN FINDINGS GATE
============================================================

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  The main hypothesis (h-main) was fully confirmed: prefill-decode disaggregation (AD) consistently lowers mean inter-token latency (ITL) at the cost of higher time-to-first-token (TTFT) compared to the aggregated baseline (AL), across all 42 tested conditions (7 arrival rates × 2 modes × 3 seeds). The ITL relationship with arrival rate follows the predicted alpha/(1-ρ) saturating curve with R² ≥ 0.94 for both modes.

  * ITL direction confirmed in all 21 rate×seed combinations: AD ITL < AL ITL, with the gap growing monotonically from 0.13ms at 10 req/s to 1.68ms at 130 req/s, matching the prediction that nonlinearity amplifies the advantage under load.
  * TTFT penalty of AD confirmed in all 21 conditions: AD TTFT exceeds AL TTFT by 4.2–9.4ms, attributed to KV-cache transfer overhead (~3.4ms) plus prefill server queueing.
  * Curve fit to alpha/(1-b·rate)+C yields R²=0.9441 (AL) and R²=0.9636 (AD); the AL R² slightly misses the 0.95 threshold, attributed to coarse grid resolution in parameter search rather than a model mismatch. The ratio of effective utilization parameters (ρ_AL/ρ_AD = 1.174) confirms AD reduces decode utilization by ~14.8%, which is the core mechanism described in the paper.
  * Zero request drops and zero preemptions observed across all conditions, indicating the simulator operated well within capacity limits for these arrival rates.
  * No surprises: all directional and qualitative predictions were confirmed. The experiment is valid and the results are clean across all seeds.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

