  Iteration 2 declared tier 2: single mechanism + multi-knob OR ablation OR dose-response on one knob
  Justification: iter 2, single mechanism (batch-size overhead amortization) + dose-response on one knob (rate). Iter-1 confirmed directions at tier 1; now characterizing the shape requires finer sampling.
  Prior tiers: iter-1=tier1
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  We are testing whether BLIS's ITL-vs-arrival-rate curve is concave/saturating (due to batch-size overhead amortization) rather than convex (as the paper's α/(1−ρ) queueing formula predicts). This is iteration 2, building on iteration 1 which confirmed ITL grows with rate but left the curve shape uncharacterized. The experiment sweeps 12 arrival rates from 1 to 150 req/s across both aggregated (AL) and disaggregated (AD) modes with 3 seeds each (72 total runs, ~3-4 min wall time).

  * h-main predicts a linear model (ITL = a + b·rate) will fit better (R² > 0.97) than the paper's α/(1−ρ) model (R² < 0.96 for AL), with the queueing model showing systematic residual curvature from fitting a convex function to concave data.
  * h-dose-response predicts the marginal ITL increase per unit rate will decrease monotonically above ~20 req/s — a saturating curve approaching a ceiling rather than exploding toward infinity — for both AL and AD modes.
  * The proposed mechanism is that BLIS's step-time formula has fixed per-step costs (weight loading β₃·tWeight, per-layer β₅·L, constant β₇) that amortize over larger batches at higher rates, making per-token ITL = F/B + c, which is concave in batch size and thus concave in rate.
  * Confirmation criteria are concrete: linear R² > 0.97 for both modes, α/(1−ρ) R² < 0.96 for AL, and monotonically decreasing marginal ITL increments above rate 20. Refutation would occur if α/(1−ρ) fits well (R² > 0.96) or marginal increments increase at high rates.
  * Design is well-scoped: 72 runs (12 rates × 2 PD modes × 3 seeds), fixed prompt/output lengths (512/128), all other parameters at documented defaults with explicit justifications. Diagnostics are clear — if the hypothesis fails, check whether fixed overhead terms are negligible or batch sizes remain too small for amortization.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

